              <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Dissemination</span>
              <textarea name="dissemination" rows="2" class="w-full border border-slate-300 rounded px-3 py-2 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-brand-500">Primary manuscript to a peer-reviewed pharmacoepidemiology journal; presentation at ICPE / ISPE Asian conference.</textarea>
            </label>
            <label class="flex flex-col gap-1">
              <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Key references (one per line)</span>
              <textarea name="references" rows="8" class="w-full border border-slate-300 rounded px-3 py-2 text-sm focus:border-brand-500 focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-brand-500">Maclure M. The case-crossover design: a method for studying transient effects on the risk of acute events. Am J Epidemiol 1991;133(2):144-53. doi:10.1093/oxfordjournals.aje.a115853
Mittleman MA, Maclure M, Robins JM. Control sampling strategies for case-crossover studies: an assessment of relative efficiency. Am J Epidemiol 1995;142(1):91-8. doi:10.1093/oxfordjournals.aje.a117550
Dharmarajan S, Lee JY, Izem R. Sample size estimation for case-crossover studies. Stat Med 2019;38:956-968. doi:10.1002/sim.8030 (sample size derived for this design; Dupont's matched case-control formula can understate what is needed.)
Janes H, Sheppard L, Lumley T. Case-crossover analyses of air pollution exposure data: referent selection strategies and their implications for bias. Epidemiology 2005;16(6):717-26. doi:10.1097/01.ede.0000181315.18836.9d
Suissa S. The case-time-control design. Epidemiology 1995;6(3):248-53. doi:10.1097/00001648-199505000-00010 (adjusts for exposure-time trend using a matched non-case control series.)
Wang S, Linkletter C, Maclure M, Dore D, Mor V, Buka S, Wellenius GA. Future cases as present controls to adjust for exposure trend bias in case-only studies. Epidemiology 2011;22(4):568-74. doi:10.1097/EDE.0b013e31821d09cd (the case-case-time-control method.)
Huang WC, Yang ASH, Tsai DHT, Shao SC, Lin SJ, Lai ECC. Association between recently raised anticholinergic burden and risk of acute cardiovascular events: nationwide case-case-time-control study. BMJ 2023;382:e076045. doi:10.1136/bmj-2023-076045; PMID 37758279. (AsPEN exemplar of the CCTC variant; hazard days -1 to -30, 30-day referent periods sampled from days -61 to -180.)
Hsieh CY, Su CC, Shao SC, Sung SF, Lin SJ, Kao Yang YH, Lai EC. Taiwan's NHIRD: past and future. Clin Epidemiol 2019;11:349-358.
Shao SC, Chan YY, Kao Yang YH, Lin SJ, Hung MJ, Chien RN, Lai CC, Lai EC. The Chang Gung Research Database. Pharmacoepidemiol Drug Saf 2019;28(5):593-600.</textarea>
            </label>
          </div>
