            ? " Under the case-case-time-control variant two case series are sized, not one: the reported odds ratio is a ratio of two odds ratios, so the variance of its logarithm is the sum of the two, and the future-case control series needs enough discordant cases in its own right. A number of cases that would suffice for a plain case-crossover does not suffice here."
            : "";
          return "A case-crossover study is sized in discordant cases. Every referent is the case's own person-time, so a case whose exposure status is the same in the hazard window as in all of its referent windows drops out of the conditional likelihood and contributes nothing. What decides the answer is: the number of cases ascertained, the probability that a case is exposure-discordant — which depends not only on how prevalent the exposure is but on how transient it is relative to the spacing of the windows, since an exposure persisting across the whole scheme leaves almost every case concordant and uninformative — the within-person correlation of exposure across windows, the number of referent windows sampled per case (" + nTxt + "), which raises efficiency with diminishing returns and never attains the efficiency of using all of the case's person-time (Mittleman 1995), and the size of the odds ratio to be detected."
            + cctc
            + " Power is therefore computed for the matched (conditional) analysis that will actually be run, from the expected number of discordant cases rather than from an event count, and with a method derived for this design: Dharmarajan et al (Stat Med 2019;38:956-968, doi:10.1002/sim.8030) show that borrowing Dupont's matched case-control formula, which is the common practice, can understate the number of cases actually required. Feasibility is confirmed before the analysis is executed as the expected number of EXPOSURE-DISCORDANT cases per data source, and as the count of cases with continuous data coverage back to the earliest day any referent window reaches; available person-time is not the constraint in a design whose referents come from the cases themselves.";
        })(),
        skip: ["ethics"],
        amendments: s.amendments,
        version: s.version,
        limitations: "The case-crossover design assumes a transient exposure and an abrupt-onset outcome with no time trend in exposure prevalence; an exposure-time trend or carry-over would bias the estimate (mitigated by a time-stratified or case-time-control variant). " + ESTIMAND_LIMIT,
        // Same trade as the SCCS builder. The shared "Participant-flow diagram
        // (source population → analytic cohort …)" printed directly above the
        // case-selection diagram below: two bullets for one artefact, and the
        // shared wording asserts a cohort a case-only design never assembles —
        // each case is their own referent, and a case whose windows agree drops
        // out of the conditional likelihood entirely. The "(with exclusions and
        // counts)" content the shared bullet carried moves here so the trade
        // loses nothing. Table 1 and the results table stay: neither is
        // duplicated here, and the forest plot is a figure, not a table shell.
        // Fills the flow slot rather than skipping it, for the same reason as
        // the SCCS builder: a selection diagram has to precede the table that
        // describes the population it selects.
        plannedOutputs: [{ key: "flow", text: "Case-selection flow diagram (source population → ascertained cases → exposure-discordant cases contributing to the likelihood, with exclusions and counts at each step)." }, "Exposure discordance across hazard / control windows.", "Odds ratio with a forest plot across sites."],
      });
      const sitesLine = (ids, otherText) => {
        const parts = (ids || []).map((c) => "- " + formatSiteFull(c));
        const others = (otherText || "").split(/\r?\n/).map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);
        if (parts.length === 0 && others.length === 0) return "_(no sites selected)_";
        let out = parts.join("\n");
        if (others.length > 0) out += (out ? "\n\n**Other sites:**\n" : "**Other sites:**\n") + others.map((o) => "- " + o).join("\n");
        return out;
      };

      const buildMarkdown = (s) => {
        const today = new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10);
        const title = orFb(s.title, "(Untitled multi-national case-crossover study)");
        const G = ccoGeom(s);
        const variant = ({ standard: "Standard (Maclure 1991); fixed offset(s) before event", "time-stratified": "Time-stratified (Janes 2005); controls drawn from same calendar stratum", symmetric: "Symmetric bidirectional; controls before AND after event", cctc: "Case-case-time-control (CCTC, Huang/Yang/Tsai BMJ 2023; PMID 37758279); current-case crossover paired with a future-case control crossover; OR_CCTC = OR_Case / OR_Control" })[s.ccoVariant] || "Standard";
        const meta = ({ re: "Random-effects (DerSimonian-Laird) meta-analysis pooling site-specific ORs; report I² and τ². Forest plot per outcome.", "re-reml": "Random-effects (REML) meta-analysis; report I² and τ²; forest plot per outcome.", fe: "Fixed-effects (inverse-variance) meta-analysis; forest plot per outcome." })[s.metaMethod] || "Random-effects meta-analysis.";
        const arch = ({ federated: "Federated; each AsPEN site executes a common analytic package locally and shares aggregate ORs.", cdm: "Common Data Model; each site transforms data into agreed CDM, runs shared package.", centralised: "Centralised; site-level individual data pooled under DUA." })[s.arch] || "Federated";

        const refsUser = (s.references || "").split(/\r?\n/).map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);
        const refs = (() => { const set = new Set(refsUser); for (const c of liveCitations(s).live) set.add(c); return Array.from(set); })();
        const milestones  = (s.timeline  || "").split(/\r?\n/).map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);
        const subgroups   = mergeList(s.subgroupsPick, s.subgroupsCustom);
        const sensitivity = mergeList(s.sensitivityPick, s.sensitivityCustom);

        return `# ${title}

**Study ID:** ${orFb(s.shortId, "(short id)")}
**Lead investigator(s):** ${orFb(s.leadInvestigator, "(lead)")}
**Version:** ${orFb(s.version, "v0.1 draft · " + today)}
**Design:** Multi-national case-crossover study

---

${window.PC.abstractMd(pcOf(s))}
## 1. Research question

${mdTable([["Population", orFb(s.picoP, "_(population)_")], ["Exposure", orFb(s.picoI, "_(exposure)_")], ["Outcome (case)", orFb(s.picoO, "_(outcome)_")]])}

## 2. Sites

${sitesLine(s.sites, s.sitesOther)}

## 3. Case definition

**Operational definition.** ${orFb(s.outcomeDef, "_(case definition)_")}

**Code set.** ${orFb(s.outcomeCodes, "_(codes)_")}

**Index date.** ${orFb(s.indexDateDef, FB.indexDate)}

## 4. Exposure definition

${mdTable([["Name", orFb(s.exposureName, "_(exposure)_")], ["Code set", orFb(s.exposureCodes, "_(codes)_")], ["Ascertainment rule", orFb(s.exposureRule, FB.exposureRule)]])}

## 5. Hazard (case) window

${hazardRows(G) ? mdTable(hazardRows(G)) : geomRefusal(G)}

## 6. Control (referent) window(s)

${G.ok ? mdTable(controlRows(G, s)) + "\n\n" + coverageLine(G) : "_See section 5._"}
${variantCaveat(s) ? "\n**Referent scheme for the variant chosen above.** " + variantCaveat(s) + "\n" : ""}
## 7. Design variant

${variant}

## 8. Design assumptions and checks

${ASSUMPTIONS.map((a, i) => (i + 1) + ". **" + a.t + ".** " + a.plain + "\n\n   *Why it matters.* " + a.precise + "\n\n   *What we do about it.* " + a.check + " [" + a.cite + "]").join("\n\n")}

${checksMd(G, s)}

**What the estimate is.** ${ESTIMAND_LIMIT}

## 9. Statistical analysis

- **Primary model:** ${orFb(s.primaryModel, FB.primaryModel)}
- **Effect estimate:** ${orFb(s.effectMeasure, FB.effectMeasure)}
- **Meta-analysis across sites:** ${meta}

**Subgroup analyses**

${bullets(subgroups)}

**Sensitivity analyses**

${bullets(sensitivity)}

## 10. Execution and timeline

